<p class="gna-lede">Recovery after jaw surgery is not random. It follows a predictable arc, and the small choices you make each day have an outsized effect on the final result.</p>
<h2 id="day">Day 0: After Surgery</h2>
<p>You wake up in the recovery room with mild inter-maxillary elastics holding the jaws in their new position. Your face feels numb, your nose feels blocked, and your throat is sore from the breathing tube. These sensations are normal and settle within hours. The maxillofacial surgery team will keep you in recovery for 3 to 6 hours, monitoring bleeding, swelling, and nausea, before moving you to a room or, in many cases, sending you home the same day or the next morning. For an overview of what happens in the operating room, see our guide to <a href="/blogs/what-is-orthognathic-surgery/">what orthognathic surgery actually involves</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Wake up in recovery with mild elastics between the jaws</li>
<li>Mild nausea from anaesthesia — usually settles in a few hours</li>
<li>Cold compress applied immediately</li>
<li>Liquid-only intake begins</li>
<li>Walking short distances the same day</li>
<li>Discharge from hospital usually within 24 to 48 hours</li>
</ul>
<p>On day 0, focus only on three things: sipping water, walking to the bathroom, and resting with your head elevated at 30 to 45 degrees. These three small acts start the healing clock and reduce the swelling that will peak later in the week.</p>
<h2 id="week1">Week 1: The First Seven Days</h2>
<p>The first week is the most physically uncomfortable phase of <strong>jaw surgery recovery</strong>, but it is also the most predictable. Swelling peaks at day 3, plateaus for 48 hours, and then begins to drop. Bruising appears around day 2 to 3, often tracking down the neck and into the upper chest before fading. Pain is usually moderate and is controlled with the prescribed analgesic schedule — do not wait for the pain to peak before taking the next dose.</p>
<ul>
<li>Swelling peaks at day 3, then begins to settle</li>
<li>Bruising appears around day 2 to 3</li>
<li>Liquid diet (smoothies, broths, protein shakes, soups)</li>
<li>Sleep propped up on 2 to 3 pillows</li>
<li>Pain controlled with prescribed medication</li>
<li>Most patients rest at home; light walking is encouraged</li>
<li>Suture check at day 5 to 7</li>
</ul>
<p>Oral hygiene during this week is non-negotiable. Use a soft baby toothbrush on the outer surfaces of the teeth only, and rinse twice a day with chlorhexidine mouthwash. Avoid spitting — let the rinse fall out gently. Most patients lose 2 to 3 kilograms in the first week simply because the liquid diet and reduced activity drop the calorie count. This is normal and recovers quickly once soft foods begin.</p>
<h2 id="week2-6">Weeks 2 to 6: Real Improvement</h2>
<p>By week 2, the swelling has dropped enough that you look recognisable again, and most patients feel ready to return to desk work or college. The elastics remain in place but are often changed to lighter guiding elastics at the week 2 review. By week 3, the surgeon will usually allow a soft-chew diet — mashed potato, scrambled egg, soft fish, well-cooked dalia, khichdi, and idli are all good options. By week 4, most bruising is gone and you begin formal jaw-opening exercises to recover mouth opening. By weeks 5 to 6, light exercise resumes, and the residual numbness in the chin and lower lip starts to recover in most patients.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Week 2:</strong> swelling clearly better. Most patients return to desk work or school</li>
<li><strong>Week 3:</strong> transition to soft-chew (mashed potato, scrambled egg, soft fish)</li>
<li><strong>Week 4:</strong> most bruising gone. Begin jaw-opening exercises</li>
<li><strong>Week 5 to 6:</strong> light exercise resumed. Most numbness starts to recover</li>
</ul>
<p>The mouth-opening exercises are the most important daily habit of this phase. Aim for 3 sets of 10 passive stretches per day, using stacked tongue depressors or your fingers to gently push the opening wider. Most patients regain 35 to 40 mm of opening by week 6 and full opening by 3 months.</p>
<h2 id="month3-12">Months 3 to 12: The Long Tail</h2>
<p>The visible swelling is largely gone by month 2 to 3, and your bite feels stable and predictable. This is the phase where the surgery recedes into the background of your life — you chew normally, speak normally, and forget you ever had plates and screws in your jaws. From month 3 to 6, any residual numbness in the chin or lower lip fades for the vast majority of patients. By month 6 to 12, the final result is fully visible, orthodontic detailing is complete, and retainers are fitted to hold the new bite in place.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Month 2 to 3:</strong> normal diet resumed. Bite fully settled</li>
<li><strong>Month 3 to 6:</strong> residual numbness fades. Chewing and speech feel natural</li>
<li><strong>Month 6 to 12:</strong> final result visible. Orthodontic detailing completed. Retainers fitted</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper look at how the bite is finalised, see our guide on <a href="/blogs/jaw-surgery-orthognathic-complete-guide-for-patients/">the complete orthognathic surgery journey for patients</a>, and for what the surgery is actually trying to achieve functionally, read <a href="/blogs/what-is-orthognathic-surgery-purpose-procedure-and-recovery-explained/">the purpose, procedure, and recovery explained</a>.</p>
<h2 id="diet">Diet Progression Chart</h2>
<p>Diet progression is the single most practical question patients ask, and it follows a fixed four-stage ladder. Liquids for the first 2 weeks, purees from week 2 to 3, soft-chew from week 3 to 6, and a near-normal diet from week 6 onward. The jaw bone is not healed at week 6 — that takes 6 to 8 weeks — but the soft tissues and the fixation plates are stable enough for normal chewing force. Avoid hard, crunchy, or chewy foods (raw carrot, toast crusts, nuts, sticky toffee) until week 8 to 10.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Week 0 to 2 (Liquids):</strong> water, broth, dal paani, fruit smoothies without seeds, protein shakes, buttermilk, thin porridge</li>
<li><strong>Week 2 to 3 (Purees):</strong> yoghurt, mashed banana, khichdi, dalia, mashed potato, blended soups</li>
<li><strong>Week 3 to 6 (Soft-chew):</strong> scrambled egg, soft fish, paneer, idli, soft pasta, well-cooked vegetables</li>
<li><strong>Week 6 onward (Normal):</strong> rice, roti (soaked), chicken, normal home-cooked meals. Avoid hard, crunchy, sticky foods until week 10</li>
</ul>
